defmodule PlaywrightEx.BrowserContext do
  @moduledoc since: "1.8"
  @moduledoc ~S"""
  BrowserContexts provide a way to operate multiple independent browser sessions.

  If a page opens another page, e.g. with a `window.open` call, the popup will belong to the parent page's browser
  context.

  Playwright allows creating isolated non-persistent browser contexts with `PlaywrightEx.Browser.new_context/2` method. Non-persistent browser
  contexts don't write any browsing data to disk.

  ```js
  // Create a new incognito browser context
  const context = await browser.newContext();
  // Create a new page inside context.
  const page = await context.newPage();
  await page.goto('https://example.com');
  // Dispose context once it's no longer needed.
  await context.close();
  ```

  ## Events

  ### `:background_page`

  *Since 1.11.* This event is not emitted.

  ### `:close`

  Emitted when Browser context gets closed. This might happen because of one of the following:

  * Browser context is closed.
  * Browser application is closed or crashed.
  * The `PlaywrightEx.Browser.close/2` method was called.

  ### `:console`

  *Since 1.34.* Emitted when JavaScript within the page calls one of console API methods, e.g. `console.log` or `console.dir`.

  The arguments passed into `console.log` and the page are available on the [`ConsoleMessage`](`PlaywrightEx.ConsoleMessage`) event handler argument.

  **Usage**

  ```js
  context.on('console', async msg => {
    const values = [];
    for (const arg of msg.args())
      values.push(await arg.jsonValue());
    console.log(...values);
  });
  await page.evaluate(() => console.log('hello', 5, { foo: 'bar' }));
  ```

  ### `:dialog`

  *Since 1.34.* Emitted when a JavaScript dialog appears, such as `alert`, `prompt`, `confirm` or `beforeunload`. Listener **must** either `PlaywrightEx.Dialog.accept/2` or `PlaywrightEx.Dialog.dismiss/1` the dialog - otherwise the page will [freeze](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/EventLoop#never_blocking) waiting for the dialog, and actions like click will never finish.

  **Usage**

  ```js
  context.on('dialog', dialog => {
    dialog.accept();
  });
  ```

  > #### Note {: .info}
  >
  > When no [`Page.on(:dialog)`](PlaywrightEx.Page.html#module-dialog) or [`BrowserContext.on(:dialog)`](PlaywrightEx.BrowserContext.html#module-dialog) listeners are present, all dialogs are automatically dismissed.

  ### `:dialog_closed`

  *Since 1.63.* Emitted when a JavaScript dialog in any page belonging to this context has been closed, either by `PlaywrightEx.Dialog.accept/2`, by `PlaywrightEx.Dialog.dismiss/1`, or manually by the user in the headed browser.

  ### `:download`

  *Since 1.60.* Emitted when attachment download started in any page belonging to this context. User can access basic file operations on downloaded content via the passed [`Download`](https://playwright.dev/docs/api/class-download) instance. See also [`Page.on(:download)`](PlaywrightEx.Page.html#module-download) to receive events about a specific page.

  ### `:frame_attached`

  *Since 1.60.* Emitted when a frame is attached in any page belonging to this context. See also [`Page.on(:frame_attached)`](PlaywrightEx.Page.html#module-frame_attached) to receive events about a specific page.

  ### `:frame_detached`

  *Since 1.60.* Emitted when a frame is detached in any page belonging to this context. See also [`Page.on(:frame_detached)`](PlaywrightEx.Page.html#module-frame_detached) to receive events about a specific page.

  ### `:frame_navigated`

  *Since 1.60.* Emitted when a frame is navigated to a new url in any page belonging to this context. See also [`Page.on(:frame_navigated)`](PlaywrightEx.Page.html#module-frame_navigated) to receive events about navigations in a specific page.

  ### `:page`

  The event is emitted when a new Page is created in the BrowserContext. The page may still be loading. The event will
  also fire for popup pages. See also [`Page.on(:popup)`](PlaywrightEx.Page.html#module-popup) to receive events about popups relevant to a specific page.

  The earliest moment that page is available is when it has navigated to the initial url. For example, when opening a
  popup with `window.open('http://example.com')`, this event will fire when the network request to "http://example.com" is
  done and its response has started loading in the popup. If you would like to route/listen to this network request, use `PlaywrightEx.BrowserContext.route/4` and [`BrowserContext.on(:request)`](PlaywrightEx.BrowserContext.html#module-request) respectively instead of similar methods on the [`Page`](`PlaywrightEx.Page`).

  ```js
  const newPagePromise = context.waitForEvent('page');
  await page.getByText('open new page').click();
  const newPage = await newPagePromise;
  console.log(await newPage.evaluate('location.href'));
  ```

  > #### Note {: .info}
  >
  > Use `PlaywrightEx.Page.wait_for_load_state/3` to wait until the page gets to a particular state (you should not need it in most
  > cases).

  ### `:page_close`

  *Since 1.60.* Emitted when a page in this context is closed. See also [`Page.on(:close)`](PlaywrightEx.Page.html#module-close) to receive events about a specific page.

  ### `:page_load`

  *Since 1.60.* Emitted when the JavaScript [`load`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Events/load) event is dispatched in any page belonging to this context. See also [`Page.on(:load)`](PlaywrightEx.Page.html#module-load) to receive events about a specific page.

  ### `:web_error`

  *Since 1.38.* Emitted when exception is unhandled in any of the pages in this
  context. To listen for errors from a particular page, use [`Page.on(:page_error)`](PlaywrightEx.Page.html#module-page_error) instead.

  ### `:request`

  *Since 1.12.* Emitted when a request is issued from any pages created through this context.
  The [request] object is read-only. To only listen for requests from a particular
  page, use [`Page.on(:request)`](PlaywrightEx.Page.html#module-request).

  In order to intercept and mutate requests, see `PlaywrightEx.BrowserContext.route/4`
  or `PlaywrightEx.Page.route/4`.

  ### `:request_failed`

  *Since 1.12.* Emitted when a request fails, for example by timing out. To only listen for
  failed requests from a particular page, use [`Page.on(:request_failed)`](PlaywrightEx.Page.html#module-request_failed).

  > #### Note {: .info}
  >
  > HTTP Error responses, such as 404 or 503, are still successful responses from HTTP standpoint, so request will complete
  > with [`BrowserContext.on(:request_finished)`](PlaywrightEx.BrowserContext.html#module-request_finished) event and not with [`BrowserContext.on(:request_failed)`](PlaywrightEx.BrowserContext.html#module-request_failed).

  ### `:request_finished`

  *Since 1.12.* Emitted when a request finishes successfully after downloading the response body. For a successful response, the
  sequence of events is `request`, `response` and `requestfinished`. To listen for
  successful requests from a particular page, use [`Page.on(:request_finished)`](PlaywrightEx.Page.html#module-request_finished).

  ### `:response`

  *Since 1.12.* Emitted when [response] status and headers are received for a request. For a successful response, the sequence of events
  is `request`, `response` and `requestfinished`. To listen for response events
  from a particular page, use [`Page.on(:response)`](PlaywrightEx.Page.html#module-response).

  ### `:service_worker`

  *Since 1.11.*

  > #### Note {: .info}
  >
  > Service workers are only supported on Chromium-based browsers.

  Emitted when new service worker is created in the context.

  Not ported yet: `clock`, `credentials`, `debugger`, `newCDPSession`, `removeAllListeners`, `storageState`, `setStorageState`.
  """

